{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## MAIN'S RED IS FIXED (change 2618) -- and my ticket's premise was wrong again\n\nsweep (macos, full suite) now PASSES, proven by workflow_dispatch against the actual schedule-gated\njob (run 33855430270): before 19243 passed + 1 ERROR; after 19244 passed, 0 errors. The +1 is the new\nregression test.\n\nMY PREMISE WAS WRONG AND THE AGENT CHECKED IT. I wrote in the ticket that the blamed test 'provisions\na real Node toolchain (npm ci plus an esbuild bundle) and is the heaviest thing in the suite'. It\ndrives a shell STUB npm ('mkdir -p node_modules; exit 0') under tmp_path and is one of the CHEAPEST\ntests in the file. Right conclusion (contention), wrong mechanism -- and my mechanism would have\naimed a fix at the test instead of the probe.\n\nThat is the SECOND time tonight an agent corrected a hypothesis I handed it, and the second time the\ncorrection came from checking the tree rather than reasoning from my summary. The first was the 2594\ndiagnosis. My briefs do say 'verify the specifics against the tree rather than re-deriving' -- that\ninstruction is earning its place, and I should treat my own hypotheses in briefs as leads rather than\nfindings, because agents visibly do defer to them.\n\nTHE REAL CAUSE, and the evidence is far better than my guess: '<Popen: returncode: -9>' shows the\nchild was ALIVE and killed by the timeout, and the same run emitted 400 [rebar-ci-timing] warnings\ntopping out at 4.562s ON A DIFFERENT WORKER -- ordinary fixture setup reaching 91% of the probe's\nfixed 5s bound. The bound sits inside the runner's own latency distribution. So the defect is THE\nBOUND, not the test: one fixed-timeout sample of a read-only idempotent probe taken ~19,000 times,\nthree xdist workers deep, across 45 minutes.\n\nReading (b) was ruled out at MECHANISM level rather than by assertion -- no real child to leak; git\nrev-parse takes no lock; a patched clock is impossible because CPython binds 'from time import\nmonotonic as _time' in subprocess; and a leaked PATH was ruled out experimentally since monkeypatch\nfinalizes before the autouse guard's post-yield.\n\nDESIGN CHOICE WORTH PRESERVING: the final attempt sits OUTSIDE the retry loop, so a persistent\ntimeout propagates unconditionally -- 'loudness is structural, not a conditional a later edit could\ninvert'. And no sleep between attempts, because the elapsed per-attempt timeout IS the backoff and\nsleeping would re-introduce the patched-time.sleep hazard _stable_subprocess_time defends against.\nBoth are the kind of decision that survives a future editor who does not know why.\n\nAC4 held: the e2e test is untouched -- not skipped, not marked, not excluded. Given how often tonight\nproduced 'absence of execution reported as success', I am glad that AC was written before the\ntemptation arose.\n\nSTATE: EIGHT of my changes now sit LLM-Review +1 / Verified -1, every one blocked solely on\nscanner-integration, none with a failure of its own: 2594, 2601, 2607, 2608, 2609, 2613, 2614, 2618.\nThat includes the fix for main's red. Main stays red until 2618 lands, and 2618 cannot land until\nanother session's 2617 does."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788515038619661001,"uuid":"52840fb2-f122-4f28-9129-a996eda7ffec"}